+# CVE-2026-76642 — False Positive Assessment for Debian Bookworm (util-linux)
+
+## Summary
+
+**CVE-2026-76642 does not affect the util-linux package shipped in Debian Bookworm.**
+The assigned impact requires privileged libmount `MNT_STAGE_MOUNT_POST` hooks
+(`X-mount.idmap`, `X-mount.owner` / group / mode) that continue to run after a
+failed external mount helper. Those hooks were introduced in util-linux **v2.39**.
+Debian Bookworm ships **2.38.1**. There is nothing to patch.
+
+---
+
+## CVE Description
+
+> A failed external mount helper in util-linux libmount can still leave the process
+> in a privileged post-mount hook path. An attacker who can cause a helper failure
+> may then reach `X-mount.idmap` / `X-mount.owner` handling and obtain unintended
+> privilege on the newly created mount.
+
+- **CVE ID:** CVE-2026-76642
+- **Advisory:** [GHSA-m25x-3hj9-m26f](https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/security/advisories/GHSA-m25x-3hj9-m26f)
+- **Affected upstream:** util-linux **v2.39 through v2.42.2**
+- **Upstream fix commits:**
+  - `f57cea130839c0af8dc0525274267ae4cfd66bbf` (orig)
+  - `a15c00a9e545aa8b9cf6ec0f888ff6c7b3eaeedc` (v2.41.6)
+- **Debian security tracker:** https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-76642
+
+---
+
+## Affected Package in Debian Bookworm
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Package | util-linux |
+| Bookworm version | `2.38.1-5+deb12u3` |
+| Upstream base | util-linux v2.38.1 |
+| Debian tracker status | Still listed as vulnerable (version-range, not yet `<not-affected>`) |
+
+---
+
+## Root Cause of the CVE
+
+The security issue is not merely that `exec_helper()` returns success on helper
+failure. The **exploitable surface** is privileged **post-mount hooks** that still
+run after that failed helper:
+
+- `X-mount.idmap` (user-namespace / id-mapping hook)
+- `X-mount.owner` / group / mode
+
+Those hooks were added in **v2.39**. The upstream fix stops the post-hook chain
+when the helper fails.
+
+---
+
+## Why v2.38.1 (Debian Bookworm) Is Not Affected
+
+### 1. The vulnerable hooks do not exist before v2.39
+
+`X-mount.idmap` / `X-mount.owner` post-hooks are not in the 2.38.1 source tree.
+Bookworm therefore cannot reach the GHSA-m25x-3hj9-m26f privilege path.
+
+### 2. Direct code verification
+
+```
+$ grep -n 'X-mount.idmap\|hook_idmap\|hook_owner' libmount/src/* 2>/dev/null
+(no idmap/owner post-hook sources)
+
+$ grep -n 'open_tree' libmount/src/*.c
+(no open_tree — also unused by this CVE)
+```
+
+`exec_helper()` exists in 2.38.1, but without the v2.39+ privileged post-hooks
+the assigned CVE impact does not apply.
+
+### 3. Debian's patch set does not introduce the vulnerability
+
+`2.38.1-5+deb12u3` does not backport v2.39 `X-mount.idmap` / owner post-hooks.
+
+---
+
+## Timeline
+
+| Date | Event |
+|---|---|
+| 2022-08-04 | util-linux v2.38.1 released (Bookworm base) |
+| 2023 | v2.39 introduces X-mount.idmap / owner post-hooks |
+| 2026 | CVE-2026-76642 assigned; fix in f57cea13 / a15c00a9 |
+
+---
+
+## Conclusion
+
+**CVE-2026-76642 is a false positive for Debian Bookworm util-linux 2.38.1-5+deb12u3.**
+
+The Debian security tracker has not yet marked bookworm `<not-affected>`. Scanners
+that follow that tracker will still flag the CVE. Suppress the finding; no code
+patch is required.
+
+---
+
+## References
+
+- Upstream orig fix: https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/commit/f57cea130839c0af8dc0525274267ae4cfd66bbf
+- v2.41.6 fix: https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/commit/a15c00a9e545aa8b9cf6ec0f888ff6c7b3eaeedc
+- GitHub advisory: https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/security/advisories/GHSA-m25x-3hj9-m26f
+- Debian security tracker: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-76642
+- Debian Bookworm package: https://packages.debian.org/bookworm/util-linux
Description: Fix CVE-2026-13595 use-after-free in libblkid nested partition probing
 The partitions list previously stored partitions in a contiguous array
 grown by reallocarray() (upstream) / realloc() (v2.38). When the array
 was reallocated to a new address, existing blkid_partition pointers
 (such as tab->parent, ls->next_parent, and local parent variables in
 nested probers) became dangling references, causing a use-after-free.
 .
 The fix changes the storage from an array of structs to an array of
 pointers, where each partition is individually allocated via calloc().
 This makes pointers stable across reallocations.
 .
 Backport notes for util-linux 2.38.1 (Debian bookworm):
  * Upstream uses reallocarray(), introduced in util-linux v2.40. v2.38
    has no shim for it in include/c.h, so the call is rewritten as
    plain realloc() with the multiplication folded into the size arg.
  * The element size in that allocation changes from
    sizeof(struct blkid_struct_partition) to sizeof(blkid_partition)
    because the array is now an array of pointers (one word each)
    rather than an array of structs.
  * A pre-existing comment typo "let start" exists in v2.38 vs upstream
    "let's start"; the patch preserves the v2.38 spelling so the
    context matches.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/util-linux/util-linux/commit/c0186f14fbdb02f64c8e0ba701ce727ea764ff4c
